# UI / views layer

Everything React-flavoured lives in `src/components/`. The editor surface is `NodeEditor.tsx`; per-processor UI lives in `flow-nodes/views/`; chrome and widgets live in `flow-nodes/`. The engine is consumed via the `EngineContext` provider — node views never import `DataflowEngine` directly.

## Top-level structure

```
src/
├── theme.ts                # MUI dark theme (Geist palette, JetBrains Mono)
├── App.tsx                 # BrowserRouter → '/' → NodeEditor
└── components/
    ├── NodeEditor.tsx       # Orchestrator
    ├── node-editor.css
    ├── node-editor/         # Editor sub-components
    │   ├── constants.ts            # GRID_SIZE, snapToGrid, nextId, SLOT_COMPAT
    │   ├── menuGroups.ts           # Add Node menu
    │   ├── NodeEditorToolbar.tsx
    │   ├── AddNodePopover.tsx
    │   └── hooks/
    │       ├── usePipelineEngine.ts
    │       ├── useSceneHistory.ts
    │       ├── useNodeEditorShortcuts.ts
    │       └── useEdgeColouring.ts
    └── flow-nodes/
        ├── nodeTypes.ts            # processor type → React component
        ├── BaseNodeShell.tsx       # chrome
        ├── EditableStepEdge.tsx    # custom orthogonal edge
        ├── NodePinSidebar.tsx      # right-side pin panel
        ├── categoryColors.ts
        ├── types.ts                # PipelineNodeData
        ├── context/                # React contexts
        ├── hooks/                  # node-side hooks
        ├── widgets/                # form widgets
        ├── styles/                 # plain CSS files (no modules)
        └── views/                  # one file per processor
```

## NodeEditor (`components/NodeEditor.tsx`)

Single page. Three concerns:

1. **State** — `useNodesState` + `useEdgesState` (React Flow), `engineRef` (DataflowEngine), `pin.state`, `fps`, `sidebarOpen`, `contextMenu`.
2. **Lifecycle** — Pixi `Application` init via `usePipelineEngine`, scene restore from Dexie via `applySnapshot` from `useSceneHistory`, FPS push to engine.
3. **User actions** — connect (`onConnect` with one-edge-per-target-handle dedupe), validate (`isValidConnection` consults `SLOT_COMPAT`), add (`addNode` from `AddNodePopover` at the right-click point), delete (`onNodesDelete`), copy/paste/undo/redo (`useNodeEditorShortcuts`), export/import/clear (own callbacks), pan/zoom (`onMoveEnd → pushSnapshot`).

`<ReactFlow>` is wrapped in `<EngineProvider>` + `<PinProvider>`. The full graph + pin sidebar live inside `node-editor-body`. Loading gate shows "Initializing GPU..." until `engineReady`. The Add Node popover snaps creation positions to the editor grid.

### `PipelineNodeData`

```ts
type PipelineNodeData = {
  processor: string                    // matches PROCESSOR_CATALOG key
  params: Record<string, unknown>
  label?: string
  imageUrl?: string                    // image processor only
  segPoints?: SamPoint[]               // segmentation processor only
  segPolygon?: number[]
  segMaskKey?: string
}
```

Engine state lives in the `DataflowEngine`; only persistence-relevant duplicates leak into node data (`imageUrl` / `segPoints` / etc.). Don't store transient runtime state in `data.params` — it'll be undo'd into staleness.

## BaseNodeShell (`flow-nodes/BaseNodeShell.tsx`)

Wraps every node view. Renders:

- `NodeResizer` (visible when selected, grid-snapped via React Flow's `snapToGrid`).
- `.pn-header` — coloured top border (driven by CSS variable `--pn-accent` set from `categoryColor(category)`).
- `.pn-handles` — input / output `<Handle>`s plus aligned labels.
- `.pn-widgets` — children area (params UI).
- `PinToggle` — adds/removes the node from the pin sidebar.

### Grid alignment (don't break this)

```
ROW_H = 20            # pitch of input/output rows (must equal GRID_SIZE)
ROW_TOP_PAD = 10      # top inset before first port
.pn-header height = 36px (in node-card.css)
.pn top border = 4px
```

The first port's centre lands at Y = 60 from the node's top edge, which is exactly 3 grid cells. Subsequent ports inherit this alignment because `ROW_H = GRID_SIZE`. Any change to header height or `ROW_TOP_PAD` must keep the first-port centre on a grid dot, otherwise edges fan off-grid.

### Headless mode

`useIsHeadlessNode()` returns `true` when the node is rendered inside the pin sidebar. In that mode `BaseNodeShell` skips:

- `<Handle>`s (no graph wiring).
- The `.pn-handles` row container.
- `NodeResizer`.

Children (widgets) still render. This is what lets a previewable processor (Preview, Log, AnimationController, ContourPreview, NoiseVisualizer) double as a "live readout" panel.

## EditableStepEdge (`flow-nodes/EditableStepEdge.tsx`)

Custom orthogonal edge with draggable bend points. Bends persist in `edge.data.bends: { x, y }[]`. Hover shows midpoint handles per straight segment that drag perpendicular to the segment direction (horizontal segment → vertical drag; vertical → horizontal). When `bends` is empty, falls back to the React Flow built-in smooth-step HVH path. Replaces the previous `reroute` waypoint node — bends are an edge property, undone/redone with the rest of the scene.

The edge is registered as `editableStep` in `node-editor/constants.ts → pipelineEdgeTypes` and added by default via `defaultEdgeOptions={{ type: 'editableStep' }}`.

## Contexts (`flow-nodes/context/`)

- **`EngineContext`** — `EngineProvider value={engine}` + `useEngine()`. Every view that subscribes to outputs or sets params reads the engine here.
- **`PinContext`** — `usePinning()` returns `{ isPinned, togglePin, pinnedIds, ... }`. Pin state is captured in snapshots, hence lifted to `usePinState({ liveNodeIds })` above the provider.
- **`NodeIdContext`** — `useResolvedNodeId()`. Returns the active node's id (React Flow's `useNodeId` in graph mode) or an override id for the pin sidebar (so the same view component can render the same node from two places).
- **`HeadlessNodeContext`** — `useIsHeadlessNode()` → boolean. Disables handles / resizer when true.

## Hooks (`flow-nodes/hooks/`)

- **`useSetParam(id)`** → `(key, value) => engine.updateNodeParams(id, { ...params, [key]: value })`. Used by every widget that drives a single param.
- **`useNodeOutputs(id)`** → `Record<string, unknown>` — subscribes to `engine.subscribeNode` and returns the latest outputs. Initial state is hydrated synchronously from `engine.getOutputs(id)` so the view sees the latest values immediately on mount (without this, non-`alwaysDirty` processors like Config would show stale data until the user touched an upstream param).
- **`useCanvasFill`** — Canvas2D sizing helper (DPR-aware). Used by the SegmentationNodeView and any preview that draws on its own canvas.

## Widgets (`flow-nodes/widgets/`)

Reusable form components, all exported through the barrel `widgets/index.ts`:

| Widget | Purpose |
|--------|---------|
| `Field` | Label + content grid (one row of the param form). |
| `NumberField` | Numeric input with `step`/`min`/`max`. |
| `TextFieldRow` | Single/multiline text input in a `Field`. |
| `SelectField` | Dropdown. |
| `SwitchField` | Boolean toggle. |
| `SliderField` | Slider + numeric readout. |
| `ColorField` | Colour picker swatch (used when `FieldDef.color = true`). |
| `ActionButton` | Primary / secondary action button. |
| `StatusLine` | Status text, three tones (`normal | muted | error`). Used for live node state. |
| `SectionTitle` | Uppercase section heading. |

Conventions:

- `slotProps={{ htmlInput: { className: 'nodrag' } }}` on every text input so dragging text doesn't pan the canvas.
- Widgets are MUI components styled via `sx` and the `node-card-mui.css` overrides scoped to `.pn`.
- Spacing unit: 6 px (`theme.spacing(1) = 6`). Buttons are 28 px tall, inputs 24 px, dense everywhere.

## Per-processor views (`flow-nodes/views/`)

Pattern (every view uses it):

```tsx
export const FooNodeView = memo(({ id, data }: NodeProps & { data: PipelineNodeData }) => {
    const set = useSetParam(id)
    const outputs = useNodeOutputs(id)            // optional, for live readouts
    return (
        <BaseNodeShell title={fooDef.title} category={fooDef.category}
                       inputs={fooDef.inputs} outputs={fooDef.outputs}
                       minWidth={...}>
            <Field label="Param">
                <NumberField value={data.params.x} onChange={v => set('x', v)} />
            </Field>
            {/* ... */}
        </BaseNodeShell>
    )
})
```

Connection hints (StatusLines like "wire X to Y") belong on **consumer** nodes only — input nodes (Text, TextStyle, TextStrip) carry no hints because they're the source side of the wire and it's not their concern to teach about downstream wiring.

Some views read the upstream Config node to get effect-specific labels (`EffectNodeView` for texture slots; `AnimationControllerNodeView` for animation slots). The pattern:

```ts
function selectUpstreamConfigId(nodeId: string) {
  return (s: ReactFlowState) =>
    s.edges.find(e => e.target === nodeId && e.targetHandle === 'config')?.source ?? ''
}
const upstreamId = useStore(useMemo(() => selectUpstreamConfigId(nodeId), [nodeId]))
const upstreamOutputs = useNodeOutputs(upstreamId)
```

This re-renders both when the wire changes (selector against `s.edges`) and when the upstream Config publishes a new effect name (subscription).

## Pin sidebar (`flow-nodes/NodePinSidebar.tsx`)

Right rail showing pinned nodes in **headless** mode. The sidebar wraps every pinned node in `HeadlessNodeContext` + `NodeIdContext`, then renders the same view components as the graph but without handles/resizer. Lets the user keep a small live-readout panel for Preview / Log / AnimationController / ContourPreview while editing the graph.

State is part of the snapshot — `pinnedIds` field on `SceneSnapshot`, restored by `useSceneHistory.applySnapshot`. Uses `localStorage["nodeEditor.sidebarOpen.v1"]` for the open/closed toggle.

## Toolbar (`node-editor/NodeEditorToolbar.tsx`)

Buttons: back link, undo/redo, export/import/clear, sidebar toggle, FPS dropdown. The FPS dropdown lists `FPS_OPTIONS` (typically `[0, 5, 10, 24, 30, 60]`) and persists to `localStorage["nodeEditor.fps.v1"]`. `0` = uncapped.

## Theme (`src/theme.ts`)

Custom dark MUI theme:

- Geist/Vercel-inspired palette.
- Monospace primary typography (JetBrains Mono Variable 700; Inter Variable as fallback).
- 6 px spacing unit.
- 28 px button height, 24 px input height.
- Dense switches/checkboxes/sliders.

`@fontsource` packages ship the variable fonts; loading happens in `main.tsx`. No Tailwind, no shadcn, no CSS-in-JS framework beyond MUI's `sx`.

## CSS conventions

- `flow-nodes/styles/` holds plain `.css` files (not modules), class-prefixed (`.pn`, `.pn-header`, `.pn-body`, `.pn-handles`, `.pn-widgets`, `.pn-resize-line`, `.pn-resize-handle`, `.pn-edge-*`).
- `node-card.css` — card chrome, header, handles, widgets, resize handles.
- `node-card-mui.css` — MUI overrides scoped under `.pn`.
- `editable-edge.css` — orthogonal edge + bend-point styles.
- `pin-sidebar.css` — sidebar card styles.

Don't add CSS modules, Emotion `styled`, or Tailwind. The dense scoping under `.pn` is intentional and makes per-processor view CSS unnecessary.

## Common pitfalls

- **Importing `DataflowEngine` directly in a view** → breaks the engine/UI separation. Use `useEngine()` and the typed handle hooks.
- **Storing transient state in `data.params`** → it'll be persisted, undone, and restored. Keep it in component state or processor instance fields.
- **Missing `nodrag` on a text input** → the canvas pans while typing.
- **Returning a new array reference from `useNodeOutputs` consumer's `useMemo`** without proper deps → unnecessary re-renders. Prefer `useNodeOutputs(id).foo` directly when only one field is needed.
- **Hard-coding `ROW_H`** in a view → if the value drifts from `GRID_SIZE` everything goes off-grid. Always `BaseNodeShell` for the chrome; if you need a fixed-pixel layout, snap to `GRID_SIZE` from `node-editor/constants.ts`.
- **Not setting `minWidth` on a wide widget area** → the resizer's default min (160) is too small for some processor views (effect, log, animationController). Pass `minWidth={...}` to `BaseNodeShell`.
- **Forgetting `memo` on a view** → React Flow re-renders all visible nodes on every interaction. The base pattern wraps the view in `memo`; do the same for any new one.
